      "item_3_ne_vymyla_že": "Unanimous: 2 (dominant prefix vy- overrides negation, roots, and enclitics)",
      "item_4_ponesla": "Unanimous: 3 (feminine mobile past ending -la is oxytone)",
      "item_5_vyvela_že": "Unanimous: 1 (dominant prefix vy- on initial syllable)",
      "item_6_i_unesli": "Heavy majority (11-1) for 4. Colleague 3 defected from 1 to 4, leaving only Colleague 4 arguing for 1."
    }
  },
  "the_item_6_debate": {
    "competing_hypotheses": {
      "Option_4_Desinential_Stress": {
        "logic": "Conjunction 'i' is an inert proclitic that never attracts stress in any attested training example ('i mylo' = 2, 'i vela' = 3, 'i ne unesla' = 5). Proclitic stress retraction is exclusively tied to the negative operator 'ne' ('ne nesli' = 1, 'ne poneslo' = 1). In affirmative mobile past forms lacking an enclitic attractor 'že', the surface stress falls on the inflectional desinence -li ('uneslí'). Syllable 4.",
        "supporters": ["Colleague 1", "Colleague 2", "Colleague 3", "Colleague 5", "Colleague 6", "Colleague 7", "Colleague 8", "Colleague 9", "Colleague 10", "Colleague 11", "Colleague 12"]
      },
      "Option_1_Leftmost_Default": {
        "logic": "Pure Zaliznyak binary model: morphemes are (+) or (-). 'nesli že' = 3 proves -li is (-). 'ne vyvelo že' = 2 proves 'ne' is (-). 'ne nesli' = 1 is therefore default phrase-initial stress on a (-) domain. 'i unesli' has only (-) morphemes, so default phrase-initial stress falls on syllable 1 ('i').",
        "supporters": ["Colleague 4"]
      }
    },
    "assessment": "The Olympiad design perspective strongly supports 4 because the prompt explicitly disclaims outside knowledge ('No knowledge of Old Russian or Russian is required...'). In self-contained linguistics competitions, assigning stress to a coordinating conjunction that never bears stress across 3 training instances is virtually never the intended answer, especially when a clear distinction between the negative particle 'ne' and the conjunction 'i' is visible. Furthermore, Old Russian accentology distinguishes clitics that take retracted stress (ne, na, po, etc.) from proclitics that are non-accentable (i, a, no). Therefore, 4 is empirically and methodologically superior within the test environment."
  },
  "target_sequence": "352314"
}

